{"ts":"2026-08-11T05:03:50.753Z","author":"pi-agent","author_source":"asserted","agent_harness":"pi","agent_model":"glm-5.2:cloud","agent_model_source":"environment","agent_instance":"f5a6c492255e087dcf4ddaf7","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"glm-5.2:cloud","source":"environment"},"effort":null,"role":null,"topic":{"value":"pm-rl-cnc0","source":"argv"}},"op":"comment_add","patch":[{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-08-11T05:03:50.753Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/comments","value":[{"created_at":"2026-08-11T05:03:50.753Z","author":"pi-agent","text":"promoteGeneration now wraps countPromotedUnderApproval + the promotion write in client.claim(approvalId), released in a finally. A lost test-and-set race -> budget_contended (CONFLICT) naming the approval item, telling the caller to retry, never falling through. Non-race claim failures (e.g. terminal approval) rethrow as-is. isAlreadyClaimedError is not a public SDK export in this version; the race is detected structurally via context.code === 'already_claimed_by' (the exact predicate the SDK helper applies). Empirical scratch-script observation on a real Decision item: claim sets assignee to the author WITHOUT changing status (open->open); release restores assignee to undefined and leaves status unchanged, so release fully restores the prior state and no explicit restore is needed. Adversarial test: Promise.allSettled over 6 real PmClients (distinct authors) promoting 6 distinct candidates against a budget of 1 asserts exactly one succeeds. Revert-proof: reverting the claim wrapper lets 4 succeed (>1) and is restored."}]}],"before_hash":"126391cb7508eb06ac3bee359f73c09aac7b2de0506f7b57af0d4342b532ca63","after_hash":"3598bf75034c7e1797ab01fdce27c0c0d66117a6b41ea4a1ae7da808cf7a97c2"}
